1
00:00:03,663 --> 00:00:12,369
Hi, I'm Neal Kleinberg, chef-owner of Clinton Street Baking Company and Restaurant, and today we're going to make the perfect brioche French toast.

2
00:00:12,369 --> 00:00:16,421
Clinton Street has been here almost 25 years.&nbsp;

3
00:00:16,421 --> 00:00:20,925
We're just known for making the most delicious breakfast and brunch foods.

4
00:00:20,925 --> 00:00:23,935
The French toast is one of our top sellers.

5
00:00:23,935 --> 00:00:27,044
On the weekends, we could sell up to 50 orders a day.

6
00:00:27,044 --> 00:00:31,129
What makes the perfect French toast is a few components.

7
00:00:31,129 --> 00:00:41,848
The batter, the garnish, the maple butter, and I'm going to show you the most important part right now, which is our brioche bread.

8
00:00:41,848 --> 00:00:43,840
[The Bread] This is our brioche bread.

9
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:54,812
It's like a white bread enriched with eggs and egg yolks and butter, and it makes it so golden and so tender and so beautiful, and that's what you're going to taste when you eat the French toast.

10
00:00:54,812 --> 00:00:58,190
And we like to slice it really nice and thick.

11
00:00:58,190 --> 00:01:02,919
Beautiful, thick slices, two inches, three inches, however you like it.

12
00:01:02,919 --> 00:01:06,132
If you can't get brioche, challah is a nice stand-in.

13
00:01:06,132 --> 00:01:18,759
The difference between challah and brioche is challah is made with oil, and it makes it very soft, but brioche is made with full butter, and you can really taste the butter in that bread when you bake it and when you cook it.&nbsp;

14
00:01:18,759 --> 00:01:28,142
If you don't have brioche and you can't get challah, I would go with a simple, good-quality white bread, a day-old French bread, but the ultimate is the brioche.

15
00:01:28,142 --> 00:01:32,842
Downstairs, we have a full bakery where we make at least 80 loaves a week.

16
00:01:32,842 --> 00:01:44,422
The shape of the brioche is called the Pullman shape or a Pullman loaf, named after the famous Pullman train cars, and they come out like a big square with a little dome on the top.

17
00:01:44,422 --> 00:01:47,396
If you're making this at home, cut the bread a little thick.

18
00:01:47,396 --> 00:01:50,031
I'm talking an inch and a half to two inches thick.

19
00:01:50,031 --> 00:01:58,748
The thickness of the bread has a lot to do with how much batter is absorbed, and when you absorb a good amount of batter, you're absorbing all the flavor.

20
00:01:58,748 --> 00:02:06,990
If I cut this brioche too thin, you're not going to get any of the fluffiness, the texture and the nuance as if you'd sliced it thick.

21
00:02:06,990 --> 00:02:10,018
This bread, right out of the oven, it's very soft.

22
00:02:10,018 --> 00:02:25,551
You need to rest it a day so that you get a really nice, firm texture in between, inside, but once you dip it in the batter, if it's a day old, it just sucks up the batter and it makes it really, really nice.

23
00:02:25,551 --> 00:02:32,135
[The Batter] Our bread is sliced, and it's time to move on to the batter.

24
00:02:32,135 --> 00:02:34,599
The ratios are very important.

25
00:02:34,599 --> 00:02:39,722
The biggest mistake people make about the batter is too much milk, not enough egg.

26
00:02:39,722 --> 00:02:42,747
Too much egg, not enough milk. It's about the balance.

27
00:02:42,747 --> 00:02:44,471
We'll start with the eggs and the cream.

28
00:02:44,471 --> 00:02:55,930
We're using half and half because heavy cream, it's too rich, so we use half and half, which is half cream and half milk, which makes it a little lighter, a little easier to work with.

29
00:02:55,930 --> 00:02:57,972
We have four eggs.

30
00:02:57,972 --> 00:03:01,868
So I would say the ratio is three parts half and half to one part egg.

31
00:03:01,868 --> 00:03:06,592
I'm going to just whisk this together, and you're going to see the color change a little bit.

32
00:03:06,592 --> 00:03:08,240
It's a look and feel as well.

33
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:16,390
Sometimes the milk and cream are a little richer. Sometimes your eggs are a little larger, and as you can see, it's starting to get a little golden in color.

34
00:03:16,390 --> 00:03:24,524
The consistency I'm looking for is the consistency of heavy cream, and I'm making sure that the whites and the yolks are whisked in really nice.

35
00:03:24,524 --> 00:03:26,469
You can't really overmix this.

36
00:03:26,469 --> 00:03:29,125
The next thing I'm going to do is add a little bit of vanilla.

37
00:03:29,125 --> 00:03:31,982
We use pure, 100% vanilla.

38
00:03:31,982 --> 00:03:37,185
Home cooks often skip the use of real vanilla extract. They'll use an imitation.

39
00:03:37,185 --> 00:03:40,124
I also like to add a little bit of vanilla paste.

40
00:03:40,124 --> 00:03:42,047
The vanilla paste is beautiful.

41
00:03:42,047 --> 00:03:47,857
It has specks of the vanilla bean in it, and it makes the batter nice and speckled.

42
00:03:47,857 --> 00:03:51,948
You can really smell the beautiful vanilla bean flavor.

43
00:03:51,948 --> 00:03:57,279
This batter should be a little bit sweet, so I'm going to add maybe a half a cup of sugar.

44
00:03:57,279 --> 00:04:00,257
Then I'm going to add a nice pinch of cinnamon.

45
00:04:00,257 --> 00:04:03,542
You want that flavor to come out in the batter.

46
00:04:03,542 --> 00:04:05,475
Got a beautiful aroma.

47
00:04:05,475 --> 00:04:07,358
I'm going to use a pinch of salt.

48
00:04:07,358 --> 00:04:15,500
The salt pulls together the seasoning of the whole thing, and then this beautiful organic lemon oil.

49
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:21,321
This is like a lemon extract made from organic lemons, and also some orange oil.

50
00:04:21,321 --> 00:04:25,275
There's a lot of nice oils coming out of the orange peel.

51
00:04:25,275 --> 00:04:32,637
The citrus is adding the nuance, pulling the whole entire flavor profile together, and I'm constantly whisking it.

52
00:04:32,637 --> 00:04:35,412
It keeps the cinnamon integrated into the batter.

53
00:04:35,412 --> 00:04:37,717
I'm going to pour the batter gently.

54
00:04:37,717 --> 00:04:41,814
You see a little bit of cinnamon straggling at the end.

55
00:04:41,814 --> 00:04:47,049
I'm just going to pour that batter right in there.

56
00:04:47,049 --> 00:04:56,293
[Soaking] When we go to dip the French toast, we're going to dip it in a square container like that, and then like that.

57
00:04:56,293 --> 00:05:05,868
See, I'm not dipping it for very long, but I'm dipping it enough so the brioche is absorbing all of that beautiful batter.

58
00:05:05,868 --> 00:05:08,790
You can even see some of the cinnamon coming up to the top.

59
00:05:08,790 --> 00:05:10,904
It's nice and soaked.

60
00:05:10,904 --> 00:05:12,463
The same with this side.

61
00:05:12,463 --> 00:05:14,001
I'll let it drain off.

62
00:05:14,001 --> 00:05:17,617
You can actually see it getting porous in here.

63
00:05:17,617 --> 00:05:23,897
You can actually see the batter entering the middle of the bread, and then you'll actually feel the weight.

64
00:05:23,897 --> 00:05:26,809
It's soaked in the middle, but it's not over-soaked.

65
00:05:26,809 --> 00:05:35,367
You know how sometimes if you're making French toast at home and you don't drain it, and you put it in your pan and you get that egg ring around? That's bad.

66
00:05:35,367 --> 00:05:42,570
And that comes from the batter itself, not having too much egg in the batter and also draining it.

67
00:05:42,570 --> 00:05:49,856
So when you go to the stove to cook it in your pan, you're not going to have that excess ring coming out of the brioche.

68
00:05:49,856 --> 00:05:53,376
One more time here. You don't want to soak this too much.

69
00:05:53,376 --> 00:05:55,467
The brioche is a very light bread.&nbsp;

70
00:05:55,467 --> 00:06:02,773
You dip it and drain it, and then you want to move to the stove.

71
00:06:02,773 --> 00:06:07,442
[Cooking] The first thing we're going to do is we're going to hit this griddle with some clarified butter.

72
00:06:07,442 --> 00:06:10,528
No milk solids in it, so it's not going to burn.

73
00:06:10,528 --> 00:06:12,919
I have the griddle set at about 350.

74
00:06:12,919 --> 00:06:19,638
If you have something at home like a cast iron pan or a thickel griddle or an electric griddle, that's great too.

75
00:06:19,638 --> 00:06:21,739
So we're going to take our dipped brioche.

76
00:06:21,739 --> 00:06:30,650
We're going to put it right on the clarified butter, and then we're going to hit it with a little more clarified butter in between just to get the nooks and crannies going.

77
00:06:30,650 --> 00:06:36,085
The clarified butter cooks it long enough, gets a crust around it without burning it.

78
00:06:36,085 --> 00:06:40,725
We're going to add a little bit of whole butter in between the French toast.

79
00:06:40,725 --> 00:06:44,113
A little knob there and a little knob there.

80
00:06:44,113 --> 00:06:47,840
This allows the French toast to get really caramelized on the outside.

81
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:50,578
The milk solids from the butter are starting to brown.

82
00:06:50,578 --> 00:06:55,065
When you're making this at home, you just don't want it to be too high of a flame.

83
00:06:55,065 --> 00:07:00,486
Medium high heat to start and then a little bit lower to finish.

84
00:07:00,486 --> 00:07:05,729
If it's too high of the flame, the butter's going to burn before you get that nice color.

85
00:07:05,729 --> 00:07:08,467
We're getting a nice little bubbly outside.

86
00:07:08,467 --> 00:07:11,225
The grill is not too hot.

87
00:07:11,225 --> 00:07:13,287
We're going to peek a little bit.

88
00:07:13,287 --> 00:07:18,239
The eggs and butter in the bread helps with the browning process.

89
00:07:18,239 --> 00:07:25,608
As you can tell, when I flip one, you've got that beautiful golden brown color.

90
00:07:25,608 --> 00:07:29,665
You can even see the cinnamon speckled in there.

91
00:07:29,665 --> 00:07:34,695
You can always check the bottom.

92
00:07:34,695 --> 00:07:37,995
And so we are gonna flip this one. That's a beautiful color.

93
00:07:37,995 --> 00:07:42,927
You see that really nice ring around that edge of the brioche?

94
00:07:42,927 --> 00:07:51,520
From the time I put it on the griddle until I flipped it, I would say two minutes. Maybe that's three minutes.

95
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:56,769
This one was a little light. Maybe I flipped it a little early, but this dish is very forgiving.

96
00:07:56,769 --> 00:08:04,760
The biggest mistake people make at home is they flip it too many times or they're not sure or they dig into this and they're like, oh my God.

97
00:08:04,760 --> 00:08:06,725
I do a little test.

98
00:08:06,725 --> 00:08:10,042
You see how it's kind of springing back to form?

99
00:08:10,042 --> 00:08:14,422
If it doesn't spring back to form, that means it's a little raw inside.

100
00:08:14,422 --> 00:08:19,544
Remember, there's raw eggs in the batter, so you have to give it enough time to cook the eggs through.

101
00:08:19,544 --> 00:08:21,013
It's kind of like a custard.

102
00:08:21,013 --> 00:08:24,646
And I like to shingle them to have a three-dimensional look.

103
00:08:24,646 --> 00:08:33,604
And this brioche is perfect because of the thick slices.

104
00:08:33,604 --> 00:08:37,132
[The Toppings] I see my pan is really, really nice and hot here.

105
00:08:37,132 --> 00:08:41,588
To make my caramelized bananas, I'm going to take some brown sugar.

106
00:08:41,588 --> 00:08:44,629
I'm going to caramelize it in the pan.

107
00:08:44,629 --> 00:08:47,749
I'm going to add a nice spoon of butter.

108
00:08:47,749 --> 00:08:50,369
This is some sweet butter, unsalted.

109
00:08:50,369 --> 00:08:56,257
And while the brown sugar is caramelizing with the butter, I'm going to add a little bit of cinnamon sugar.

110
00:08:56,257 --> 00:09:00,802
We want to take a ripe banana, cut it in big, thick chunks.

111
00:09:00,802 --> 00:09:07,564
You see it nice and smoky and caramelized, the sugar, the cinnamon, and then we're going to add our bananas right to the top.

112
00:09:07,564 --> 00:09:12,813
We're swirling them around and tossing them in the caramel to get them nice and coated.

113
00:09:12,813 --> 00:09:15,184
And then we're going to kind of put it off to the side.

114
00:09:15,184 --> 00:09:17,617
The bananas don't need to cook too much.

115
00:09:17,617 --> 00:09:21,004
Once that's started, we're going to make our maple butter.

116
00:09:21,004 --> 00:09:24,195
And we're basically just going to heat up a cup.

117
00:09:24,195 --> 00:09:27,089
There's no substitute for real maple syrup.

118
00:09:27,089 --> 00:09:30,299
The beauty of this sauce, it doesn't take a lot of time.

119
00:09:30,299 --> 00:09:33,975
Just heat it on the low to medium heat.

120
00:09:33,975 --> 00:09:38,753
Add spoons of butter to this sauce while you're whisking it.

121
00:09:38,753 --> 00:09:48,004
It's basically like a French butter sauce where you're constantly whisking and you're constantly adding butter until it's dissolved.

122
00:09:48,004 --> 00:09:57,875
As I make this sauce, the color gets lighter and lighter from a dark amber to a light butterscotch kind of color.

123
00:09:57,875 --> 00:09:59,369
Our sauce is where I want it.

124
00:09:59,369 --> 00:10:05,148
And we're going to garnish the French toast with some beautiful Texas pecans.

125
00:10:05,148 --> 00:10:07,357
This is just a small little nonstick pan.

126
00:10:07,357 --> 00:10:09,775
And I just like to give them a little toast.

127
00:10:09,775 --> 00:10:12,991
It releases a lot of the oils and the flavors of the nuts.

128
00:10:12,991 --> 00:10:15,418
It gives it a nice crunchy top.

129
00:10:15,418 --> 00:10:19,401
You can toast these well in advance and then kind of toss them.

130
00:10:19,401 --> 00:10:22,966
One of the things that you don't want to do is burn them.

131
00:10:22,966 --> 00:10:26,434
Now I'd like to go and plate the final dish.

132
00:10:26,434 --> 00:10:28,953
So with the garnish here, our caramelized bananas.

133
00:10:28,953 --> 00:10:32,245
We basically just want to put them on the dish naturally.

134
00:10:32,245 --> 00:10:34,578
You see how beautiful?

135
00:10:34,578 --> 00:10:37,929
Then we're going to put some of our toasted pecans over the top.

136
00:10:37,929 --> 00:10:42,421
We're going to pour some of our maple butter right out of the pitcher.

137
00:10:42,421 --> 00:10:46,942
And then that last shake of the cinnamon sugar is like the most important thing for me.

138
00:10:46,942 --> 00:10:49,728
And here's our signature brioche French toast.

139
00:10:49,728 --> 00:10:51,733
To me, this is one of my favorite bites.

140
00:10:51,733 --> 00:10:53,135
It is so soothing.

141
00:10:53,135 --> 00:10:54,200
It is so comforting.

142
00:10:54,200 --> 00:11:01,734
It's something you want to just eat on a Sunday morning with a cup of coffee.

143
00:11:01,734 --> 00:11:05,217
The inside of the French toast is perfect.

144
00:11:05,217 --> 00:11:07,492
I see a little bit of moistness in here.

145
00:11:07,492 --> 00:11:11,101
I know the batter is cooked in the middle, but it's still nice and soft.

146
00:11:11,101 --> 00:11:15,318
I can see the nooks and crannies of the actual brioche.

147
00:11:15,318 --> 00:11:17,365
So I know it wasn't over soaked.

148
00:11:17,365 --> 00:11:21,109
Still firm, yet spongy texture.

149
00:11:21,109 --> 00:11:26,553
I'm now tasting all of the little nuances and profiles of the flavors.

150
00:11:26,553 --> 00:11:31,189
I could taste and smell the aroma of the lemon and the orange coming out.

151
00:11:31,189 --> 00:11:34,493
I get a real nice, deep flavor of the vanilla.

152
00:11:34,493 --> 00:11:35,844
It's not too sweet.

153
00:11:35,844 --> 00:11:40,740
It's just the balance of the savory, the butteriness of the bread.

154
00:11:40,740 --> 00:11:43,346
The bananas can really taste the banana.

155
00:11:43,346 --> 00:11:45,341
The nuttiness of the pecans.

156
00:11:45,341 --> 00:11:49,437
The cinnamon sugar at the very end gives it a nice little sandy texture.

157
00:11:49,437 --> 00:11:51,936
All of those things make the perfect French toast.

158
00:11:51,936 --> 00:11:53,870
It's all about experience.

159
00:11:53,870 --> 00:12:04,222
It's all about your personal taste, how you like things, and how you know your friends and family like it when you make it.

160
00:12:04,222 --> 00:12:06,722
If I had to choose between love and butter, I would choose butter.

